# One module, two files. This file defines a function `added`; the test file
# beside it defines a constant `added`. Two top-level declarations with one
# name in one module should be refused at compile time, the way a parameter
# or a local that reuses a top-level name is ("`q` is already a declaration;
# rename the binding").
#
# Actual: `kanso check` and `kanso test` accept the module, and the test
# fails at run time with
#   error[runtime]: `5` is not callable
#     --> .:22:3
# The call `added acc 1` below reached the test file's constant instead of
# this function, and the location names the module, not this file.
#
# Run: kanso test bugs/constant_hides_function
pub fn total xs
  bump 0 xs

fn added acc n
  acc + n

fn bump acc xs
  added acc (length xs)
